\section{Baseline is explicit}{
Stata's \code{schedule()} option lists follow-up visits only and assumes an implicit
baseline at time 0. This port requires baseline to be given explicitly, because
the implicit convention is a common source of off-by-one design errors. A
\code{visits} vector that does not start at 0 is rejected with a suggested correction
rather than silently repaired.
}

\section{Real-valued visit times}{
Stata restricts \code{schedule()} to ascending integers of at least 1 and provides a
\code{scale()} option to compensate, because it builds the covariance matrix on a
unit-integer grid. This port builds it directly at the requested times, so
\code{scale()} is unnecessary and has no equivalent: express \code{visits} in whatever
units the fitted slope uses.
}

\section{Incremental versus cumulative dropout}{
The two conventions describe the same strata from opposite ends, and confusing
them changes the trial design materially. A participant counted in
\code{dropout[j]} (incremental) attends \code{visits[1:j]} and misses everything after,
so their last attended visit is \code{visits[j]} and the first they miss is
\code{visits[j + 1]}. The print method shows both columns so the reading is never
ambiguous.

Participants whose last attended visit is baseline contribute no follow-up
measurement and therefore no information about the slope. They are excluded
from the calculation entirely, and \code{trial_design()} warns when \code{dropout[1]} is
non-zero so that this is visible rather than silent.
}

